ML_PAGE("t2-notes", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l2\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">TOPIC 2 · COMPLETE REFERENCE</span><h2>Full Intuitive Explanation</h2><p>The complete Topic 2 narrative, preserved in full. Use the guided lessons for a first pass; open this when a concept still isn't clicking.</p></header><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l2\" data-action=\"print\">Print this page</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"t2-subjective\">Go to subjective prep</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"t2-prep\">Go to MCQ prep</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l2\" id=\"t2n-toc\"><summary><strong>Reading order</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">44 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"1. Start with the business itself: legal structure changes risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s1\" title=\"1. Start with the business itself: legal structure changes risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Legal structure changes risk</button></li><li data-full=\"2. Sole proprietorship: business risk can become personal risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s2\" title=\"2. Sole proprietorship: business risk can become personal risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Sole proprietorship</button></li><li data-full=\"3. Partnership: risk is shared-but responsibility becomes more complicated\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s3\" title=\"3. Partnership: risk is shared-but responsibility becomes more complicated\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Partnership</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Limited liability fundamentally changes the risk boundary\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s4\" title=\"4. Limited liability fundamentally changes the risk boundary\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Limited liability (Sdn. Bhd.)</button></li><li data-full=\"5. LLP: why does it exist?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s5\" title=\"5. LLP: why does it exist?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>LLP</button></li><li data-full=\"6. Corporations separate ownership from control\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s6\" title=\"6. Corporations separate ownership from control\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Ownership vs control</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Business structures should therefore be analysed as risk allocation systems\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s7\" title=\"7. Business structures should therefore be analysed as risk allocation systems\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Structures as risk allocation</button></li><li data-full=\"8. Now connect the organisation to its objectives\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s8\" title=\"8. Now connect the organisation to its objectives\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Now connect the organisation to its objectives</button></li><li data-full=\"9. These objectives can conflict\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s9\" title=\"9. These objectives can conflict\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>These objectives can conflict</button></li><li data-full=\"10. Shareholder wealth is not simply today&#x27;s profit\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s10\" title=\"10. Shareholder wealth is not simply today&#x27;s profit\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Shareholder wealth vs profit</button></li><li data-full=\"11. Single bottom line vs Triple Bottom Line\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s11\" title=\"11. Single bottom line vs Triple Bottom Line\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Single bottom line vs Triple Bottom Line</button></li><li data-full=\"12. Enterprise risks exist at different organisational levels\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s12\" title=\"12. Enterprise risks exist at different organisational levels\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Four levels of enterprise risk</button></li><li data-full=\"13. Strategic risk = &quot;Are we doing the right thing?&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s13\" title=\"13. Strategic risk = &quot;Are we doing the right thing?&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Strategic risk</button></li><li data-full=\"14. Tactical risk = &quot;How do we execute the strategy?&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s14\" title=\"14. Tactical risk = &quot;How do we execute the strategy?&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Tactical risk</button></li><li data-full=\"15. Operational risk = &quot;Can the organisation execute day to day?&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s15\" title=\"15. Operational risk = &quot;Can the organisation execute day to day?&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Operational risk</button></li><li data-full=\"16. Compliance risk = &quot;Are we allowed to do what we are doing?&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s16\" title=\"16. Compliance risk = &quot;Are we allowed to do what we are doing?&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Compliance risk</button></li><li data-full=\"17. These levels interact\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s17\" title=\"17. These levels interact\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>These levels interact</button></li><li data-full=\"18. What is risk management really?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s18\" title=\"18. What is risk management really?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>What is risk management really?</button></li><li data-full=\"19. Risk management is a decision-making system\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s19\" title=\"19. Risk management is a decision-making system\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>Risk management is a decision-making system</button></li><li data-full=\"20. Risk management has pre-loss AND post-loss objectives\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s20\" title=\"20. Risk management has pre-loss AND post-loss objectives\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Pre-loss and post-loss objectives</button></li><li data-full=\"21. Now we reach the heart of the chapter: evolution of risk management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s21\" title=\"21. Now we reach the heart of the chapter: evolution of risk management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Evolution of risk management</button></li><li data-full=\"22. Stage 1 - Insurance-based risk management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s22\" title=\"22. Stage 1 - Insurance-based risk management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>Stage 1 - Insurance-based risk management</button></li><li data-full=\"23. Stage 2 - Traditional risk management: risks handled in silos\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s23\" title=\"23. Stage 2 - Traditional risk management: risks handled in silos\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>Stage 2 - Traditional risk management</button></li><li data-full=\"24. The silo problem\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s24\" title=\"24. The silo problem\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>The silo problem</button></li><li data-full=\"25. Correlation changes everything\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s25\" title=\"25. Correlation changes everything\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>Correlation changes everything</button></li><li data-full=\"26. Corporate Risk Management initially focused heavily on financial risks\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s26\" title=\"26. Corporate Risk Management initially focused heavily on financial risks\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>CRM focused on financial risks</button></li><li data-full=\"27. Stage 3 - Integrated Risk Management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s27\" title=\"27. Stage 3 - Integrated Risk Management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>Stage 3 - Integrated Risk Management</button></li><li data-full=\"28. Stage 4 - Enterprise Risk Management (ERM)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s28\" title=\"28. Stage 4 - Enterprise Risk Management (ERM)\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>Stage 4 - Enterprise Risk Management (ERM)</button></li><li data-full=\"29. Traditional RM vs ERM\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s29\" title=\"29. Traditional RM vs ERM\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>Traditional RM vs ERM</button></li><li data-full=\"30. Why ERM is about value, not simply protection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s30\" title=\"30. Why ERM is about value, not simply protection\"><span class=\"toc-no\">30</span>Why ERM is about value, not simply protection</button></li><li data-full=\"31. ERM should not aim to eliminate all risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s31\" title=\"31. ERM should not aim to eliminate all risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">31</span>ERM should not aim to eliminate all risk</button></li><li data-full=\"32. Stage 5 - Holistic Risk Management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s32\" title=\"32. Stage 5 - Holistic Risk Management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">32</span>Stage 5 - Holistic Risk Management</button></li><li data-full=\"33. Why sustainability enters the risk discussion\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s33\" title=\"33. Why sustainability enters the risk discussion\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>Why sustainability enters the risk discussion</button></li><li data-full=\"34. Risk categories evolve because the business environment evolves\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s34\" title=\"34. Risk categories evolve because the business environment evolves\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>Risk categories evolve</button></li><li data-full=\"35. Technology changes risk management itself\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s35\" title=\"35. Technology changes risk management itself\"><span class=\"toc-no\">35</span>Technology and risk management</button></li><li data-full=\"36. A full integrated example\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s36\" title=\"36. A full integrated example\"><span class=\"toc-no\">36</span>A full integrated example</button></li><li data-full=\"37. Higher-order scenario: why silo management can actively mislead management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s37\" title=\"37. Higher-order scenario: why silo management can actively mislead management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">37</span>Silos can mislead management</button></li><li data-full=\"38. Another important distinction: risk owner vs risk manager\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s38\" title=\"38. Another important distinction: risk owner vs risk manager\"><span class=\"toc-no\">38</span>Risk owner vs risk manager</button></li><li data-full=\"39. The chapter&#x27;s evolutionary logic in one sentence\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s39\" title=\"39. The chapter&#x27;s evolutionary logic in one sentence\"><span class=\"toc-no\">39</span>The evolution in one sentence</button></li><li data-full=\"40. Bloom&#x27;s Taxonomy - what different exam questions actually require\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s40\" title=\"40. Bloom&#x27;s Taxonomy - what different exam questions actually require\"><span class=\"toc-no\">40</span>Bloom's levels for Topic 2</button></li><li data-full=\"41. A difficult exam-style case\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s41\" title=\"41. A difficult exam-style case\"><span class=\"toc-no\">41</span>A difficult exam-style case</button></li><li data-full=\"42. Evaluate the decision, don&#x27;t merely identify risks\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s42\" title=\"42. Evaluate the decision, don&#x27;t merely identify risks\"><span class=\"toc-no\">42</span>Evaluate, don't just identify</button></li><li data-full=\"43. The most important distinction in Topic 2\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s43\" title=\"43. The most important distinction in Topic 2\"><span class=\"toc-no\">43</span>The key distinction</button></li><li data-full=\"44. The whole chapter compressed into one mental map\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2n-s44\" title=\"44. The whole chapter compressed into one mental map\"><span class=\"toc-no\">44</span>One mental map</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l2\" open><summary><div><strong>Topic 2 - complete intuitive explanation</strong><small>Every section of the notes, with worked examples and interactive visuals.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>44 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><p>Topic 2 is more conceptually important than it first appears. At surface level, the slides look like a mixture of business structures, corporate objectives, risk-management definitions, and a history lesson. The deeper idea tying everything together is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Risk management evolved because organisations themselves became larger, more complex, more interconnected, and exposed to risks that could no longer be managed independently.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is the lens I would use for the entire chapter. The chapter explicitly covers business types and their associated risks, the origins of risk management, its principles/aims, and its evolution.</p>\n<p>A useful master framework is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Business Structure → Objectives → Risk Exposures → Risk Management → Evolution from Silos to ERM\"><span>Business Structure</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Objectives</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Risk Exposures</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Risk Management</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evolution from Silos to ERM</span></div>\n<p>The exam-worthy question is therefore not merely:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"What is ERM?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Why did traditional risk management become insufficient, and how does ERM solve that problem while supporting corporate objectives?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is where this chapter becomes interesting.</p>\n<hr /><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. Start with the business itself: legal structure changes risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_structures\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Legal form moves the boundary of who absorbs the loss; it does not remove business risk.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The first major section deals with different forms of business organisation.</p>\n<p>For Malaysia, the slides identify five main forms:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Sole proprietorship  </li>\n<li>Partnership  </li>\n<li>Limited Liability Partnership (LLP)  </li>\n<li>Private Limited Company (Sdn. Bhd.)  </li>\n<li>Public Limited Company (Berhad/Bhd.)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Do not treat this as a business-law memorisation exercise.</p>\n<p>The real risk-management question is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Who ultimately bears the consequences when something goes wrong?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Sole proprietorship: business risk can become personal risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t2_liability_sim\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Where does the loss stop?</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Pick a legal form and the size of the debts; see who pays: the business, the owners personally, or the creditors.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The slide describes a sole proprietor as having <strong>unlimited liability</strong>: the owner is personally liable for the firm's debts and obligations.</p>\n<p>Suppose Ammar owns:</p>\n<p><strong>Ammar Coffee Enterprise</strong></p>\n<p>The business has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM100,000 \\text{ assets}$$</div>\n<p>but incurs:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM500,000 \\text{ liabilities}$$</div>\n<p>If the business cannot pay, the problem does not necessarily stop at the shop.</p>\n<p>Because liability is unlimited:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Business loss → owner&#x27;s personal exposure\"><span>Business loss</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>owner&#x27;s personal exposure</span></div>\n<p>The conceptual distinction is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Owner and business are not strongly separated</span></div>\n<p>The slide on page 6 reinforces this by showing single ownership, one person's capital, one-person control and unlimited liability.</p>\n<p>This structure has obvious advantages:</p>\n<p>fast decisions,</p>\n<p>few formalities,</p>\n<p>direct control,</p>\n<p>flexibility.</p>\n<p>But the same features produce risk concentration.</p>\n<p>If one person provides:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>capital + management + expertise + decision making</span></div>\n<p>then that person becomes a <strong>single point of failure</strong>.</p>\n<p>If the owner:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>becomes incapacitated,</li>\n<li>makes a bad strategic decision,</li>\n<li>cannot obtain financing,</li>\n<li>leaves the business,</li>\n</ul>\n<p>the firm may have very little resilience.</p>\n<p>So the risk perspective is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Simplicity reduces administrative complexity but increases concentration risk.</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. Partnership: risk is shared-but responsibility becomes more complicated</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides describe partnerships as involving at least two people and requiring an agreement to become partners.</p>\n<p>More people can bring:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{more capital}\n+\n\\text{more expertise}\n+\n\\text{shared workload}$$</div>\n<p>which sounds safer.</p>\n<p>But now you have a new source of risk:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>another human being</span></div>\n<p>Perhaps not quite the revolution management textbooks advertise.</p>\n<p>The slides say partners can have unlimited liability and may be jointly and severally liable for partnership debts.</p>\n<p>So imagine two partners:</p>\n<p>Ali and Bala.</p>\n<p>Bala makes a disastrous business commitment.</p>\n<p>The partnership loses RM1 million.</p>\n<p>Ali may still face substantial liability even if:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"But Bala did it.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>From a risk perspective:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Partnership}\n=\n\\text{shared resources}\n+\n\\text{shared decision risk}\n+\n\\text{potential liability exposure}$$</div>\n<p>You therefore gain diversification of skills but introduce:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>agency problems,</li>\n<li>conflict,</li>\n<li>decision disagreements,</li>\n<li>partner misconduct,</li>\n<li>continuity risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Limited liability fundamentally changes the risk boundary</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides describe Sdn. Bhd. shareholders as generally liable only for the amount of their investment, and the company as a separate legal entity.</p>\n<p>This creates an important boundary:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Company} \\neq \\text{Shareholder}}$$</div>\n<p>Suppose you invest:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM100,000$$</div>\n<p>into a company.</p>\n<p>The company suffers:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM5m \\text{ losses}$$</div>\n<p>Your economic exposure is generally limited to your investment rather than automatically becoming a RM5 million personal debt.</p>\n<p>This does <strong>not</strong> mean:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"The company has less risk.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The company's operational, credit, strategic and market risks may be exactly the same.</p>\n<p>What changed is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Who bears the financial consequence?</span></div>\n<p>This is a much better way to analyse legal structures.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. LLP: why does it exist?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Your slides present an LLP as combining the simplicity of partnership with legal protection similar to a company.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{LLP}\n\\approx\n\\text{Partnership flexibility}\n+\n\\text{Limited liability}$$</div>\n<p>Hence it can be attractive to professional firms.</p>\n<p>So when comparing structures, don't ask only:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Which structure has limited liability?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What trade-off is the structure trying to solve?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For an LLP:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Flexibility vs legal protection</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. Corporations separate ownership from control</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_ownership_control\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Limited liability and professional management solve concentration risk but open an agency gap.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Now we get into something far more important for enterprise risk management.</p>\n<p>The slides state that corporations are responsible for their own obligations, and owners are generally not personally liable for corporate obligations.</p>\n<p>But page 14 introduces another feature:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Shareholders → Board of Directors → President/Management → Functional executives\"><span>Shareholders</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Board of Directors</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>President/Management</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Functional executives</span></div>\n<p>This creates separation between:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Ownership</span></div>\n<p>and</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Control</span></div>\n<p>Shareholders own the firm.</p>\n<p>Management runs the firm.</p>\n<p>That creates an entire new family of risks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>governance risk,</li>\n<li>agency risk,</li>\n<li>managerial misconduct,</li>\n<li>information asymmetry,</li>\n<li>poor oversight,</li>\n<li>strategic misalignment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Suppose shareholders want:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{long-term shareholder value}$$</div>\n<p>but management wants:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>short-term bonus maximisation</span></div>\n<p>Those objectives can conflict.</p>\n<p>So corporations solve some risks through limited liability but create new risks through organisational complexity.</p>\n<p>That is an important theme of this chapter:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Solving one risk can create another.</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Business structures should therefore be analysed as risk allocation systems</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Page 18 compares the forms in terms of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>number of owners,</li>\n<li>liability,</li>\n<li>whether owners manage,</li>\n<li>continuity when ownership changes,</li>\n<li>taxation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Instead of memorising the table, analyse what every column means.</p>\n<h4>Liability</h4>\n<p>Who absorbs losses?</p>\n<h4>Management</h4>\n<p>Who controls decisions?</p>\n<h4>Ownership change</h4>\n<p>What happens to continuity?</p>\n<h4>Number of owners</h4>\n<p>How concentrated is capital and decision authority?</p>\n<h4>Taxation</h4>\n<p>What financial structure does the entity face?</p>\n<p>That is Bloom's <strong>analysis</strong> level.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. Now connect the organisation to its objectives</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_objective_tensions\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Dashed red lines mark goals that conflict; which one dominates decides what counts as risk.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter then asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What is the company actually trying to achieve?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Your slides list objectives including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>survival,</li>\n<li>beating competitors,</li>\n<li>maximising sales,</li>\n<li>maximising profit,</li>\n<li>minimising costs,</li>\n<li>maximising market share,</li>\n<li>maximising shareholder wealth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is where Topic 1 connects directly.</p>\n<p>Recall:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risk}\n=\n\\text{effect of uncertainty on objectives}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"No clear objective → poor risk identification\"><span>No clear objective</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>poor risk identification</span></div>\n<p>If your objective is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Maximise market share}$$</div>\n<p>then aggressive pricing may be sensible.</p>\n<p>If your objective is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Maximise short-term profit}$$</div>\n<p>the same strategy may look undesirable.</p>\n<p>If your objective is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Survival}$$</div>\n<p>cash preservation may dominate both.</p>\n<p>So <strong>risk is conditional on strategy</strong>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. These objectives can conflict</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t2_objective_tradeoff\"><div class=\"vis-title\">One decision, three objectives</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Cut prices or maintenance and judge the decision against different corporate objectives.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is where you should start evaluating rather than just repeating the slides.</p>\n<p>Consider:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Maximise sales}$$</div>\n<p>A company could slash prices.</p>\n<p>Sales volume rises.</p>\n<p>Excellent?</p>\n<p>Not necessarily.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Sales}\\uparrow$$</div>\n<p>but:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Profit margin}\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>Similarly:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Minimise costs}$$</div>\n<p>might encourage:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>fewer staff,</li>\n<li>less maintenance,</li>\n<li>cheaper suppliers,</li>\n<li>reduced quality control.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Short-term costs fall.</p>\n<p>But:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Operational Risk}\\uparrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Quality Risk}\\uparrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Reputation Risk}\\uparrow$$</div>\n<p>This gives an important ERM principle:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk management is not independent from strategic decision making.</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. Shareholder wealth is not simply today's profit</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slide highlights maximising shareholder wealth.</p>\n<p>A simplistic interpretation would be:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Maximise this year's earnings.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>But consider two firms.</p>\n<h4>Company A</h4>\n<p>Profit this year:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM1b$$</div>\n<p>but obtained by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>neglecting maintenance,</li>\n<li>exploiting suppliers,</li>\n<li>creating regulatory violations,</li>\n<li>destroying customer trust.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Company B</h4>\n<p>Profit:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM800m$$</div>\n<p>but:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>stronger controls,</li>\n<li>sustainable operations,</li>\n<li>long-term investment,</li>\n<li>trusted customers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Which has more long-term value?</p>\n<p>Not automatically A.</p>\n<p>This brings us to the chapter's next idea.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. Single bottom line vs Triple Bottom Line</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Page 21 contrasts the traditional financial bottom line with a Triple Bottom Line framework:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>People + Planet + Profit</span></div>\n<p>The visual on page 22 connects this with sustainability and the Sustainable Development Goals.</p>\n<p>Conceptually, the shift is:</p>\n<h4>Traditional view</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Success} = \\text{financial performance}$$</div>\n<h4>Broader view</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Success}\n=\nf(\\text{economic, social, environmental performance})$$</div>\n<p>This matters for risk because environmental and social problems eventually may become:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{regulatory risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{reputation risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{legal risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{supply-chain risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{financial risk}$$</div>\n<p>So sustainability is not automatically a charitable side project.</p>\n<p>In risk-management terms, it can affect:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>long-term enterprise value</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. Enterprise risks exist at different organisational levels</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_risk_levels\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Risk viewed at four levels, from direction-setting down to the rules the firm must follow.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Page 23 is one of the most important diagrams in the whole chapter.</p>\n<p>It presents risk vertically:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Strategic Risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Tactical Risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Operational Risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Compliance Risk}$$</div>\n<p>and explains that risk can be viewed at different levels to facilitate management and aggregation.</p>\n<p>Let's make this intuitive.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. Strategic risk = \"Are we doing the right thing?\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Strategic risk deals with choices that affect objectives and the direction of the enterprise.</p>\n<p>Suppose Proton decides:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"We will become an EV-first manufacturer.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That decision creates uncertainty around:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>customer demand,</li>\n<li>technology,</li>\n<li>battery supply,</li>\n<li>competition,</li>\n<li>financing,</li>\n<li>regulation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Strategic risk is therefore about:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>whether the chosen direction succeeds</span></div>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>entering China,</p>\n<p>acquiring a competitor,</p>\n<p>launching EVs,</p>\n<p>changing business model,</p>\n<p>expanding internationally.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. Tactical risk = \"How do we execute the strategy?\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose the strategic objective is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Become Malaysia's leading EV company.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Tactical decisions could include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>financing the expansion,</li>\n<li>choosing suppliers,</li>\n<li>pricing products,</li>\n<li>choosing marketing channels,</li>\n<li>IT systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your slides put credit, market, IT, marketing and competitive risk at this level.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Strategy} = \\text{Where are we going?}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Tactics} = \\text{How are we going there?}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. Operational risk = \"Can the organisation execute day to day?\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now assume the strategy and plan are good.</p>\n<p>The factory still has to work.</p>\n<p>Employees need to show up.</p>\n<p>Systems need to run.</p>\n<p>Products need to be produced.</p>\n<p>So operational risk concerns:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>daily execution</span></div>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>machine failure,</p>\n<p>human error,</p>\n<p>IT outage,</p>\n<p>fraud,</p>\n<p>supply interruption.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. Compliance risk = \"Are we allowed to do what we are doing?\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This covers legal and regulatory requirements.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>environmental regulation,</p>\n<p>employment law,</p>\n<p>financial reporting rules,</p>\n<p>licensing,</p>\n<p>safety standards.</p>\n<p>A brilliant strategy executed perfectly can still fail if it is illegal.</p>\n<p>Hence:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Strategic success}\n\\neq\n\\text{ignoring compliance}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. These levels interact</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t2_level_sorter\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Strategic, tactical, operational or compliance?</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Decide which level each risk belongs to, then reveal.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is where higher-level answers distinguish themselves.</p>\n<p>Suppose management decides to launch an EV model.</p>\n<h4>Strategic risk</h4>\n<p>Will consumers want it?</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<h4>Tactical risk</h4>\n<p>Can the company secure financing and batteries?</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<h4>Operational risk</h4>\n<p>Can factories produce reliably?</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<h4>Compliance risk</h4>\n<p>Does the battery meet safety and environmental requirements?</p>\n<p>If one level fails, the others can be affected.</p>\n<p>So enterprise risk is not a tidy four-box diagram in the real world.</p>\n<p>It is an interconnected system.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. What is risk management really?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_rm_cycle\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Risk management loops: monitoring feeds back into identification as risks change.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Your slide defines risk management as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>the process of identifying, assessing and controlling threats to an organisation's capital and earnings.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Another slide expands it into a logical and systematic method of:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Identifying → Analysing → Treating → Monitoring\"><span>Identifying</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Analysing</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Treating</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Monitoring</span></div>\n<p>risk.</p>\n<p>The cycle on page 24 shows roughly:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1.\\ Identify$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$2.\\ Analyse/Prioritise$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$3.\\ Evaluate$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$4.\\ Track/Report$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$5.\\ Control/Treat$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$6.\\ Monitor$$</div>\n<p>The crucial word is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>cycle</span></div>\n<p>Risk management is not:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"We made a risk register in January. Done.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Risks change.</p>\n<p>So monitoring feeds back into identification.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">19. Risk management is a decision-making system</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t2_risk_appetite\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Risk appetite and capacity</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Set risk appetite and capacity; ERM picks the best return that fits, not automatically the safest.</figcaption></figure></div><p>One of the best statements in the slides is that risk management improves decision making and helps managers make better use of resources.</p>\n<p>That changes the philosophy completely.</p>\n<p>Bad interpretation:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Risk management = stop risky things.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Better interpretation:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Risk management = make better decisions under uncertainty.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Suppose Project A gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Expected\\ Return = 25\\%$$</div>\n<p>with significant risk.</p>\n<p>Project B gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Expected\\ Return = 5\\%$$</div>\n<p>with low risk.</p>\n<p>Risk management does <strong>not</strong> automatically say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Choose B because safer.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That would be risk avoidance masquerading as expertise.</p>\n<p>Instead, management evaluates:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risk}$$</div>\n<p>against:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Expected opportunity/reward}$$</div>\n<p>and asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Is the risk acceptable relative to our objectives and capacity?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">20. Risk management has pre-loss AND post-loss objectives</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_pre_post_loss\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Pre-loss objectives prepare; post-loss objectives keep the firm alive and growing after the event.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is extremely examinable.</p>\n<p>Your slides divide objectives into:</p>\n<h4>Pre-loss objectives</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>prepare economically,</li>\n<li>reduce anxiety,</li>\n<li>satisfy legal obligations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Post-loss objectives</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>ensure survival,</li>\n<li>maintain operations,</li>\n<li>stabilise earnings,</li>\n<li>maintain growth,</li>\n<li>minimise effects on people/society.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Think of fire.</p>\n<h3>Before fire</h3>\n<p>Install:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>smoke detectors,</li>\n<li>training,</li>\n<li>insurance,</li>\n<li>sprinklers,</li>\n<li>evacuation systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That's pre-loss thinking.</p>\n<h3>After fire</h3>\n<p>Now management asks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Can operations resume?</li>\n<li>Can employees be paid?</li>\n<li>Can customers still be served?</li>\n<li>Can the company survive?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That's post-loss thinking.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>RM is not just loss prevention</span></div>\n<p>It also concerns:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>resilience and recovery</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">21. Now we reach the heart of the chapter: evolution of risk management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_rm_evolution\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Risks accumulated rather than disappeared, so the management approach had to widen.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter gives this broad progression:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Insurance → Traditional RM → Integrated RM → ERM → Holistic RM\"><span>Insurance</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Traditional RM</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Integrated RM</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>ERM</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Holistic RM</span></div>\n<p>This progression is far more important than memorising dates.</p>\n<p>Each stage evolved because the previous stage became insufficient.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">22. Stage 1 - Insurance-based risk management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Historically, the slides say risk management was narrowly insurance-based. Insurance mainly addressed pure risks such as fire and flood, while many uninsurable risks received much less attention.</p>\n<p>The mindset was:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Bad event → Financial loss → Insurance pays\"><span>Bad event</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Financial loss</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Insurance pays</span></div>\n<p>This works quite well for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>fire,</li>\n<li>theft,</li>\n<li>accidents,</li>\n<li>property damage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>But what about:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>competitor disruption?</li>\n<li>reputation?</li>\n<li>bad strategy?</li>\n<li>cyberattack?</li>\n<li>obsolete technology?</li>\n<li>changing customer behaviour?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You cannot simply call an insurer and say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Our management completely misunderstood the market. Please reimburse us.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Hence insurance alone was insufficient.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">23. Stage 2 - Traditional risk management: risks handled in silos</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>As organisations became more sophisticated, different departments began managing different risks.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>Finance:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>FX + interest rate + liquidity</span></div>\n<p>Insurance department:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{property + liability}$$</div>\n<p>IT:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{cyber + systems}$$</div>\n<p>Operations:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{process + safety}$$</div>\n<p>Your slides explicitly describe traditional approaches as managing risks in silos and concentrating particularly on physical hazards and financial risks.</p>\n<p>At first glance this seems sensible.</p>\n<p>Specialists manage specialist risks.</p>\n<p>So what's wrong?</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">24. The silo problem</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t2_silo_vs_erm\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Silo view versus enterprise view</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Four departments each report a 10% chance of a loss. Turn up the shared dependency and watch the chance of several hitting at once.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Imagine four managers separately conclude:</p>\n<h4>Treasury</h4>\n<p>Foreign-exchange risk = acceptable.</p>\n<h4>Operations</h4>\n<p>Supplier risk = acceptable.</p>\n<h4>Strategy</h4>\n<p>International expansion risk = acceptable.</p>\n<h4>Finance</h4>\n<p>Debt level = acceptable.</p>\n<p>Individually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_1 &lt; \\text{limit}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_2 &lt; \\text{limit}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_3 &lt; \\text{limit}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_4 &lt; \\text{limit}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore safe?</p>\n<p>Not necessarily.</p>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Total enterprise risk} \\neq \\sum \\text{of isolated conclusions}}$$</div>\n<p>What if all four depend on China?</p>\n<p>Chinese demand falls.</p>\n<p>At the same time:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>supplier problems occur,</li>\n<li>RMB moves,</li>\n<li>expansion fails,</li>\n<li>debt servicing becomes difficult.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The risks are <strong>correlated</strong>.</p>\n<p>Silo management can miss that.</p>\n<p>This is the conceptual reason ERM exists.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">25. Correlation changes everything</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_A = \\text{Supply chain risk}$$</div>\n<p>and</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_B = \\text{Market risk}$$</div>\n<p>If independent, the combined exposure might be manageable.</p>\n<p>But suppose both are caused by:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{geopolitical conflict}$$</div>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(R_A \\cap R_B)$$</div>\n<p>can become much more important.</p>\n<p>In plain English:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The risks may blow up at the same time.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A silo structure may fail to see that because each department says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"My risk is fine.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>ERM asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"What happens to the enterprise when these risks interact?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">26. Corporate Risk Management initially focused heavily on financial risks</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides describe conventional Corporate Risk Management (CRM) as focusing especially on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>credit risk,</li>\n<li>market risk,</li>\n<li>liquidity risk,</li>\n</ul>\n<p>using tools such as insurance, futures, options and hedging, often under a silo approach.</p>\n<p>This makes sense historically.</p>\n<p>Financial risk is relatively quantifiable.</p>\n<p>You can measure:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{interest rate exposure}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{FX exposure}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{credit default probability}$$</div>\n<p>But organisations eventually realised:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A company can hedge its currency perfectly and still go bankrupt because its strategy is terrible.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is why the scope widened.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">27. Stage 3 - Integrated Risk Management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides describe the 1980s-1990s as moving toward an integrated approach in which risk management became more closely connected with strategic planning and included operational, reputational and strategic risks.</p>\n<p>The difference is subtle but crucial.</p>\n<p>Traditional approach:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Strategy first}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>Risk department deals with problems</span></div>\n<p>Integrated approach:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk considerations become part of strategic decisions</span></div>\n<p>Suppose management asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Should we enter Indonesia?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Old model:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Management decides yes.</li>\n<li>Risk team insures the assets.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Integrated model:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>What is the opportunity?</li>\n<li>What strategic risks arise?</li>\n<li>What regulatory risks?</li>\n<li>What FX exposure?</li>\n<li>What supply risks?</li>\n<li>What reputational consequences?</li>\n<li>Should we still enter?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Completely different quality of decision.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">28. Stage 4 - Enterprise Risk Management (ERM)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Your slides describe ERM as a value-based management approach providing a <strong>holistic framework for measuring and managing corporate risks to create long-term firm value</strong>.</p>\n<p>That definition contains three critical ideas:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Holistic</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Enterprise-wide</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Value creation</span></div>\n<p>ERM is therefore not:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Risk management but with more spreadsheets.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It represents a fundamentally different philosophy.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">29. Traditional RM vs ERM</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_crm_vs_erm\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Conventional CRM manages financial risks in separate boxes; ERM widens the spectrum and manages it as one.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Think of traditional RM as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{array}{c}\n\\text{Finance risk}\\\\\n\\text{Operations risk}\\\\\n\\text{IT risk}\\\\\n\\text{Compliance risk}\n\\end{array}$$</div>\n<p>with relatively separate management.</p>\n<p>ERM says:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>How do all these risks combine to affect enterprise objectives?</span></div>\n<p>Your page 38 diagram captures exactly this shift:</p>\n<h4>Conventional CRM</h4>\n<p>Credit risk</p>\n<p>Liquidity risk</p>\n<p>Market risk</p>\n<p>handled separately.</p>\n<h4>New orientation / ERM</h4>\n<p>those risks</p>\n<p>+</p>\n<p>Operational risks</p>\n<p>+</p>\n<p>other risks identified through mapping</p>\n<p>managed through:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>expanded spectrum + holistic integrated approach</span></div>\n<p>The slide explicitly says ERM requires both wider identification and holistic management.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">30. Why ERM is about value, not simply protection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is perhaps the most important higher-order conclusion in the chapter.</p>\n<p>Traditional mindset:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk = bad</span></div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risk management} = \\text{reduce risk}$$</div>\n<p>ERM mindset:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk = uncertainty around objectives</span></div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risk management}\n=\n\\text{optimise decisions under uncertainty}$$</div>\n<p>Imagine two strategies:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Expected Return</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Risk</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">4%</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">15%</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Moderate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>ERM doesn't automatically choose A.</p>\n<p>It asks whether B's risk is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>understood,</li>\n<li>within capacity,</li>\n<li>within appetite,</li>\n<li>controllable,</li>\n<li>compensated by sufficient expected return.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is why page 39 visually depicts the evolution from:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>risk as something bad,</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>toward:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>uncertainty that management must respond to strategically.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">31. ERM should not aim to eliminate all risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This follows logically.</p>\n<p>If a company eliminates:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{all risk}$$</div>\n<p>it probably eliminates:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{investment}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{innovation}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{expansion}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{new products}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{entrepreneurship}$$</div>\n<p>as well.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{No risk} \\neq \\text{optimal management}}$$</div>\n<p>The aim is closer to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>appropriate risk for appropriate expected value</span></div>\n<p>That is why risk management and corporate finance are tightly connected.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">32. Stage 5 - Holistic Risk Management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Page 32 gives another progression:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Period</th>\n<th>Approach</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Early 20th century</td>\n<td>Traditional RM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1980s-1990s</td>\n<td>Integrated RM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Early 2000s</td>\n<td>Enterprise RM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recent years</td>\n<td>Holistic RM</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The slide describes recent holistic risk management as integrating risk with sustainability, governance, compliance and broader business functions.</p>\n<p>Page 35 adds a key idea:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Risks are interconnected, so managing them in silos can create missed opportunities and greater exposure.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"ERM → enterprise-wide risk integration\"><span>ERM</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>enterprise-wide risk integration</span></div>\n<p>while the more recent holistic view pushes further toward:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk + Strategy + Governance + Sustainability + Compliance</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">33. Why sustainability enters the risk discussion</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides argue that global risk concerns increasingly shifted toward social and environmental issues and that emerging risks pushed organisations toward more effective ERM frameworks.</p>\n<p>Think about climate change.</p>\n<p>It might create:</p>\n<h4>Physical risk</h4>\n<p>Flood destroys facility.</p>\n<h4>Supply-chain risk</h4>\n<p>Supplier cannot operate.</p>\n<h4>Market risk</h4>\n<p>Consumers change preferences.</p>\n<h4>Regulatory risk</h4>\n<p>Carbon rules tighten.</p>\n<h4>Financial risk</h4>\n<p>Insurance premiums rise.</p>\n<h4>Reputation risk</h4>\n<p>Company is criticised.</p>\n<h4>Strategic risk</h4>\n<p>Existing business model becomes uncompetitive.</p>\n<p>So asking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Is climate change an environmental risk?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>is almost too simplistic.</p>\n<p>It is better represented as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Environmental driver → multiple enterprise risks\"><span>Environmental driver</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>multiple enterprise risks</span></div>\n<p>That is precisely why holistic thinking matters.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s34\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">34. Risk categories evolve because the business environment evolves</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_risk_universe\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Each era added risk categories on top of the old ones.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Page 40 gives a useful sequence:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Insurance Risk → Market/Financial Risk → Operational Risk → Strategic Risk\"><span>Insurance Risk</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Market/Financial Risk</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Operational Risk</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Strategic Risk</span></div>\n<p>The important idea is not that old risks disappeared.</p>\n<p>They accumulated.</p>\n<p>Modern firms still face:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{fire risk}$$</div>\n<p>but now also:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{cyber}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{AI/model risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{ESG}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{reputation}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{global supply chains}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{geopolitics}$$</div>\n<p>So the risk universe expanded.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">35. Technology changes risk management itself</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_tech_double_edge\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Analytics improve detection and modelling but bring model, cyber, data and dependency risk.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Page 36 states that data analytics, AI and machine learning increasingly support:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>risk identification,</li>\n<li>assessment,</li>\n<li>monitoring,</li>\n<li>risk modelling,</li>\n<li>scenario analysis,</li>\n<li>real-time monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This creates an interesting double effect.</p>\n<p>Technology can:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>reduce risk</span></div>\n<p>by improving detection.</p>\n<p>But technology also creates:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>new risk</span></div>\n<p>such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>model risk,</li>\n<li>cyber risk,</li>\n<li>data-quality risk,</li>\n<li>dependency risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So again:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>A control can itself become a source of risk.</span></div>\n<p>That is a more advanced ERM insight.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s36\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">36. A full integrated example</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose <strong>AirAsia considers launching a major new route network into a new region</strong>.</p>\n<p>Analyse it using this chapter.</p>\n<h4>Corporate objective</h4>\n<p>Increase market share and shareholder wealth.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<h4>Strategic risk</h4>\n<p>Will the market be profitable?</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<h4>Tactical risks</h4>\n<p>Aircraft financing,</p>\n<p>fuel hedging,</p>\n<p>marketing,</p>\n<p>FX exposure.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<h4>Operational risks</h4>\n<p>Crew availability,</p>\n<p>aircraft maintenance,</p>\n<p>IT systems,</p>\n<p>airport capacity.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<h4>Compliance risks</h4>\n<p>Aviation regulations,</p>\n<p>landing rights,</p>\n<p>safety requirements.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<h4>Reputation risk</h4>\n<p>Delays or safety incidents.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<h4>Financial consequences</h4>\n<p>Revenue,</p>\n<p>cash flow,</p>\n<p>liquidity,</p>\n<p>shareholder value.</p>\n<p>A silo approach might let:</p>\n<p>Finance analyse FX,</p>\n<p>Operations analyse aircraft,</p>\n<p>Legal analyse regulation,</p>\n<p>Marketing analyse demand.</p>\n<p>ERM asks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>If several assumptions fail simultaneously, what happens to the enterprise?</span></div>\n<p>That is the difference.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s37\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">37. Higher-order scenario: why silo management can actively mislead management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_silo_map\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Silo reports can all be accurate and still miss the shared driver that makes them fail together.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose a Malaysian manufacturer reports:</p>\n<h4>Finance department</h4>\n<p>Currency risk:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$LOW$$</div>\n<h4>Procurement</h4>\n<p>Supplier risk:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$LOW$$</div>\n<h4>Strategy</h4>\n<p>China exposure:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$LOW$$</div>\n<h4>Logistics</h4>\n<p>Shipping risk:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$LOW$$</div>\n<p>Management sees four green boxes.</p>\n<p>Looks comforting.</p>\n<p>Unfortunately, all four depend on:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>stable China-US trade relations</span></div>\n<p>A geopolitical shock causes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{FX volatility}$$</div>\n<p>+</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{supplier disruption}$$</div>\n<p>+</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{tariffs}$$</div>\n<p>+</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{shipping disruption}$$</div>\n<p>All four risks materialise together.</p>\n<p>The problem was not necessarily bad departmental analysis.</p>\n<p>The problem was:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>failure to understand dependency</span></div>\n<p>That's a textbook reason for enterprise-wide risk aggregation.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s38\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">38. Another important distinction: risk owner vs risk manager</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_owner_vs_manager\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Owners manage the risks they create; the risk function supports and challenges; the board oversees.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The slides say risk managers identify, analyse and mitigate risks affecting organisational objectives and operations.</p>\n<p>But don't interpret that as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"The risk department owns all risks.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Consider machinery failure.</p>\n<p>Who best understands the machinery?</p>\n<p>Operations.</p>\n<p>Consider liquidity.</p>\n<p>Who understands the cash position?</p>\n<p>Treasury/finance.</p>\n<p>So in practice, the risk function helps establish:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{framework}\n+\n\\text{measurement}\n+\n\\text{challenge}\n+\n\\text{reporting}$$</div>\n<p>while business units often manage the risks they generate.</p>\n<p>Otherwise you get the absurd organisational model where everyone takes risks and then tells the risk department:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Congratulations, this is your problem now.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s39\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">39. The chapter's evolutionary logic in one sentence</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t2_stage_quiz\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Which stage is this firm at?</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Read what each firm does and name its stage of risk-management evolution.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is the sentence I would memorise conceptually rather than word-for-word:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Corporate risk management evolved from reactive insurance-based treatment of individual losses, through departmental and financial risk management, toward integrated enterprise-wide management of interconnected financial, operational, strategic, compliance, reputational, sustainability and emerging risks in support of long-term organisational value.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That essentially explains pages 27-40.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s40\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">40. Bloom's Taxonomy - what different exam questions actually require</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_bloom_ladder\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Marks rise as the question moves from listing business forms to designing an ERM structure.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Remember</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>List five Malaysian business entities.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You reproduce:</p>\n<p>sole proprietor,</p>\n<p>partnership,</p>\n<p>LLP,</p>\n<p>Sdn. Bhd.,</p>\n<p>Bhd.</p>\n<p>Easy.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Understand</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Explain unlimited liability.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You explain that business obligations may expose the owner's personal assets.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Apply</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A sole proprietor owes RM500,000 after the business collapses. Identify the liability implications.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You apply the legal structure.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Analyse</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Explain why conventional corporate risk management may fail when different departments independently manage credit, liquidity and operational risks.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now you discuss:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{silos}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{dependencies}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{correlation}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{lack of aggregation}$$</div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Evaluate</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Should a rapidly expanding manufacturer continue using a traditional risk-management approach or adopt ERM?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You should compare them.</p>\n<p>You might argue:</p>\n<p>Traditional RM may remain efficient for narrow, well-defined exposures, but as the organisation grows, risk dependencies and strategic complexity make ERM more suitable.</p>\n<p>That is much stronger than:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"ERM is better because holistic.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h3>Create</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Design a risk-management structure for a multinational company entering a new market.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You could construct:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Corporate objectives}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risk identification}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>Strategic + Tactical + Operational + Compliance assessment</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risk owners}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Cross-risk aggregation}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Treatment}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Monitoring + Reporting}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Board/Management review}$$</div>\n<p>That is actual ERM thinking.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s41\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">41. A difficult exam-style case</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t2_acquisition_cascade\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Treating the chain, not the silo</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Add treatments to the ABC Berhad acquisition and watch the residual risk of the whole chain fall.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>ABC Berhad wants to enter Indonesia by acquiring a local manufacturer. The acquisition is funded substantially through debt. The target company uses an outdated IT system, depends on one major supplier, and recently received environmental complaints. Management nevertheless expects the acquisition to increase market share by 20%.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Don't answer with one risk.</p>\n<p>Break it apart.</p>\n<h4>Objective</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Increase market share/shareholder value</span></div>\n<h4>Strategic risk</h4>\n<p>The acquisition may fail to create the expected value.</p>\n<h4>Financial/liquidity risk</h4>\n<p>Large debt financing creates repayment pressure.</p>\n<h4>Operational risk</h4>\n<p>Outdated IT may disrupt operations.</p>\n<h4>Supplier risk</h4>\n<p>Single-source dependency.</p>\n<h4>Compliance/environmental risk</h4>\n<p>Environmental complaints may create regulatory exposure.</p>\n<h4>Reputational risk</h4>\n<p>Stakeholder criticism could affect the parent company.</p>\n<h4>Enterprise interaction</h4>\n<p>Suppose environmental regulation shuts down the supplier.</p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Supplier failure → Production stops\"><span>Supplier failure</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Production stops</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow\n\\text{Revenue falls}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow\n\\text{Debt pressure increases}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow\n\\text{Liquidity risk rises}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow\n\\text{Acquisition strategy fails}$$</div>\n<p>That final chain is the ERM answer.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s42\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">42. Evaluate the decision, don't merely identify risks</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose the exam asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Should ABC abandon the acquisition?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A weak answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Yes, because there are many risks.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's not risk management.</p>\n<p>A stronger answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Not necessarily. Management should evaluate whether the expected strategic value of the acquisition justifies the aggregated exposure and whether the major risks can be reduced to within acceptable levels.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Potential treatments:</p>\n<p>supplier diversification,</p>\n<p>IT integration plan,</p>\n<p>environmental due diligence,</p>\n<p>lower leverage,</p>\n<p>liquidity reserve,</p>\n<p>post-acquisition governance controls.</p>\n<p>Then management compares:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Residual Risk}$$</div>\n<p>against:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Expected Strategic Return}$$</div>\n<p>That's <strong>Evaluate</strong> level.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s43\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">43. The most important distinction in Topic 2</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Topic 1 taught:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>What risk is</span></div>\n<p>Topic 2 now teaches:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>How organisations organise themselves to manage it</span></div>\n<p>And the entire historical progression is essentially:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Individual risks}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Departmental risks}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Integrated risks}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Enterprise-wide interconnected risks</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2n-s44\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">44. The whole chapter compressed into one mental map</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_concept_map\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Top row: the organisation and its risk. Bottom row: how managing that risk evolved.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Use this for revision:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>BUSINESS STRUCTURE</span></div>\n<p>determines ownership, liability, control and continuity.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>CORPORATE OBJECTIVES</span></div>\n<p>survival, profit, growth, market share, shareholder wealth, broader sustainability considerations.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>RISK</span></div>\n<p>uncertainty threatens or changes achievement of those objectives.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>RISK LEVELS</span></div>\n<p>Strategic → Tactical → Operational → Compliance.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>RISK MANAGEMENT</span></div>\n<p>Identify → Assess/Analyse → Evaluate → Treat → Report → Monitor.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>PRE-LOSS + POST-LOSS</span></div>\n<p>Prevent/prepare + survive/recover.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>TRADITIONAL RM</span></div>\n<p>Insurance + financial risk + silo management.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>INTEGRATED RM</span></div>\n<p>Risk becomes part of strategic planning.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ERM</span></div>\n<p>All material risks considered together in relation to enterprise objectives and long-term value.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>HOLISTIC RM</span></div>\n<p>ERM expands further into governance, sustainability, compliance, technology and interconnected emerging risks.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>The deepest lesson from Topic 2 is therefore not <strong>\"ERM manages more types of risk.\"</strong> That's true but shallow.</p>\n<p>The stronger interpretation is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ERM exists because the effect of one risk cannot always be understood independently of other risks or independently of corporate strategy.</span></div>\n<p>A modern organisation therefore needs to understand <strong>risk interactions, aggregate exposure, strategic trade-offs and long-term value</strong>, rather than merely insure isolated losses after someone in a department notices them.</p></div></details></div></details>");
